  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124472,
      "id": "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30",
      "title": "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "9b3a6bef-fa7a-542e-aa98-6ce0fa54bc0b",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/86d1b02bb44cd95c30944bb1229be9bb6c5dadca06eda4bfa654466d273a6636.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nEach graph shows its entire domain; an open circle excludes its endpoint.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nGraph positions: **I.** upper left; **II.** upper right; **III.** lower left; **IV.** lower right.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/268478d6640d3d5c32970476573a17a52f9cbe85f12e8d585dc24c7dd1b8f416.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graph I, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, this graph shows a one-to-one function. As a result, this function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs II, III, IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "b5ecd244-0402-565a-a015-b337b400ed26",
