    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4eff1904-d766-5750-86a5-28483cb88c50",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/fb52e707cc91a66af41adcdef8fb6e0d7e307ff41a77a20d9abeee87b7ba3fd4.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nEach graph shows its entire domain; an open circle excludes its endpoint.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nGraph positions: **I.** upper left; **II.** upper right; **III.** lower left; **IV.** lower right.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/d8208c9383425e830c6521821238c1e5607e684b8e59c4881233e37efb436513.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graphs I, II, III, IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.",
