      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/338541cd2e00b57e415d93962fa9546a766de5489dfd3f62407f51a89220f6b6.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graphs I, II, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, these graphs show one-to-one functions. As a result, these functions are invertible.\n\n- For graphs III, IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.",
